# p_a7e3 — Weekend: both home, mid-morning cycling, relaxed afternoon

Yuki (early fifties, office worker) and Omar (late twenties, afternoon-to-night shift) are both off work on weekends. Yuki sleeps in until roughly 9:30–10; Omar is up by 7 and makes breakfast. They share a cycling trip in the late morning, roughly 10:30 to 13:00, after which they come home, run errands around midday, and spend the rest of the day around the house—cooking together, watching TV, reading. The Saturday message confirms: "we're off our usual routine and around the house more."

This document differs from the weekday documents in three ways. First, Yuki's work items (laptop, keys, wallet, sunglasses, backpack, notebook, water bottle) are **in the house** all day; there is no 8–17 out-of-house window. Second, Omar's work items (keys, handbag, jacket, lunchbox) are also in the house; there is no 14–23 out-of-house window. Third, during the 10:30–13:00 cycling window, both residents' helmets, bike locks, water bottles, outerwear, and cycling shoes are out of the house. Outside that window everything is back at its usual resting place.

This hypothesis is refuted if Yuki's laptop or keys are found out of the house on a weekend, if both helmets are at the entry hook during 10:30–13:00 on a Saturday, or if Omar's keys are out of the house on a weekend morning.

